{:frontmatter
 {:id "adr-2607031700-kotoba-lang-engine-wasm-emission-and-host-runtime"
  :title "ADR-2607031700: kotoba-lang/engine WASM byte-emission + kotoba-lang/kami-script-runtime-rs (the recovered WASM host substrate)"
  :status "accepted"
  :doc_type "adr"
  :topic "kotoba-lang-clj-wgsl-runtime-closure"
  :authoritative true
  :last_verified "2026-07-03"
  :authoritative_for
  ["kotoba.engine-clj.wasm-bytes (kotoba-lang/engine, PR #1) is the byte-emission
    step that closes the gap between kotoba.engine-clj's Module IR (semantic
    compile, already-ported from deleted Rust codegen.rs) and real loadable
    .wasm bytes -- ported 1:1 from the deleted Rust codegen.rs::compile
    (kami-engine@a8368f9c0d784dbc9d11e8fa8f407aa95c7ce4fa:kami-engine-clj/src/codegen.rs)"
   "PR #2 fixed a real semantic bug PR #1 introduced (:f32-const encoded the
    raw Clojure float literal instead of its num/f32-bits IEEE-754 bit
    pattern) -- found only by kami-script-runtime-rs's real tick-loop
    execution, not by wasm-tools validate (structurally valid, semantically
    wrong -- any 4 bytes are valid IEEE-754)"
   "kotoba-lang/kami-script-runtime-rs is a new repo hosting the WASM HOST
    substrate (wasmtime + wasmi backends, 14 host-import bindings, entity
    store) recovered 1:1 from kami-engine's deleted
    kami-script-runtime/src/lib.rs -- this Rust substrate was ALWAYS
    classified :stay-Rust-substrate in ADR-2607010930's original ledger but
    was accidentally swept into the wholesale kami-engine Rust-workspace
    deletion; kotoba-lang/kami-script-runtime (the CLJC-scoped repo) only
    ported the genuinely-portable input_map.rs submodule and explicitly,
    permanently excludes the host substrate from its own scope -- this ADR
    is the missing repo that closes that gap"
   "isekai-network's real games/01-netsurvivors/logic.clj compiles through
    the full pipeline end-to-end and was verified actually running: entity
    count grows via spawn, the beat-gated bonus mechanic fires, pixel-level
    on-screen colour verification (via a real render-to-texture screenshot
    capture) confirms shiro-pico/ghost accent colours match
    character-design-spec.json's canonical palette after accounting for the
    shader's glow-brightening + sRGB-encode pipeline"]
  :related
  ["CLAUDE.md"
   "90-docs/adr/2607010930-clj-wgsl-migration.md"
   "90-docs/adr/2607023200-isekai-network-standalone-repo.md"
   "manifest/repos.edn"
   "kotoba-lang/engine PR #1, PR #2 (both merged)"
   "kotoba-lang/kami-script-runtime-rs (new repo)"
   "gftdcojp/isekai-network"]
  :supersedes []
  :superseded_by []}

 :problem
 {:source "session-long thread: isekai-network (gftdcojp/isekai-network,
            Ghost Hacker: Shiro & Pico) had a real, EDN-validated,
            proven-vocabulary-checked games/01-netsurvivors/logic.clj since
            early in the session, but no way to verify it actually ran --
            kami-engine-clj (the compiler CLAUDE.md documented) turned out
            to have been deleted upstream (kotoba-lang/kotoba PR #259) as
            part of ADR-2607010930's clj-wgsl migration, and its CLJC
            replacement (found under the differently-named kotoba-lang/engine)
            only produced a Module IR data structure, not loadable .wasm
            bytes -- and even with bytes, nothing existed to actually load
            and execute them with real host-import behaviour"
  :investigated
  ["kotoba.engine-clj/compile-file successfully compiled isekai-network's
    real logic.clj to a Module IR with all 8 expected functions, 14
    host-imports, correctly-evaluated constants -- confirmed via direct
    testing before any new code was written"
   "the Module IR's byte-emission gap was explicitly documented in
    kotoba-lang/engine's own README (\"a future host-adapter package is
    expected to do this\") -- not a surprise discovery, a known, scoped-out
    TODO"
   "the deleted Rust kami-script-runtime/src/lib.rs (2042 lines) was fully
    recoverable from kami-engine's git history at the same pinned SHA
    codegen.rs was recovered from, confirming both halves of the deleted
    Rust workspace share one recoverable snapshot"
   "kotoba-lang/kami-script-runtime's own README states the host-runtime
    exclusion in absolute terms (\"permanent scope exclusion, not a TODO\"),
    confirming no other repo was quietly planning to cover this -- a real
    gap, not a duplication risk (kotoba-lang/wasm-ui, kotoba-lang/webgpu,
    kotoba-lang/ui, kotoba-lang/webgl, kotoba-lang/gpu were all separately
    surveyed and confirmed unrelated: browser-only DOM/WebGPU UI substrate
    or empty Phase-4 scaffolds, none targeting native windowed wasmtime
    hosting)"]
  :risk "porting Rust->CLJC codegen semantics correctly does not guarantee
         the separate mechanical byte-emission step is correct -- WASM
         binary format bugs (wrong section order, wrong opcode, wrong
         immediate encoding) can be structurally valid (wasm-tools validate
         passes) while being semantically wrong, as PR #2's f32-const bug
         demonstrated concretely"}

 :decision
 {:byte_emission
  {:location "kotoba-lang/engine, new namespace kotoba.engine-clj.wasm-bytes"
   :method "1:1 port of the deleted Rust codegen.rs::compile's section
            assembly (type/import/function/memory/global/export/code/data,
            same order, same cabi_realloc bump-allocator special case),
            recovered read-only from kami-engine's git history at the pin
            already used for codegen.cljc's own recovery"
   :verification "wasm-tools validate (independent external validator, not
                   self-consistency) on both a minimal example and
                   isekai-network's real logic.clj; wasmtime run --invoke
                   actually executing emitted bytes and returning the
                   correct value; 51 tests / 213 assertions"
   :bug_found_and_fixed "PR #2: :f32-const missing num/f32-bits conversion,
                          found by kami-script-runtime-rs's real tick-loop
                          execution (ghosts spawned via a def-bound constant
                          moved correctly; ghosts using an inline f32
                          literal in the same cond sat at a NaN position
                          forever) -- fixed with 2 new regression tests
                          decoding the emitted bytes back via an independent
                          ByteBuffer read"}
  :host_runtime
  {:new_repo {:path "kotoba-lang/kami-script-runtime-rs"
              :visibility :public
              :naming_note "no stronger established -rs-suffix convention
                             was found in kotoba-lang for \"thin Rust
                             substrate paired with an almost-same-named CLJC
                             sibling\" at the time this repo was created --
                             flagged, not resolved (see :naming_review below)"}
   :scope "wasmtime (JIT) + wasmi (no-JIT/interpreted) dual backends, one
           binding codebase, 14 host-import implementations recovered 1:1
           from the deleted lib.rs, hand-rolled HashMap-based entity store
           (NOT hecs -- disclosed simplification, hecs recovery was not
           attempted), render/audio/physics host-imports NOT implemented
           because isekai-network's actual compiled module declares none
           (confirmed empirically via the parity test's clean run, not
           assumed) -- matches the original pre-deletion Rust's own no-op
           status for those bindings"
   :verification "wasmi/wasmtime parity test runs isekai-network's real
                   compiled module through both backends for 200 ticks,
                   asserts bit-for-bit identical position/velocity/tag/count
                   state -- the golden-frame determinism contract
                   ADR-2607010930's original kami-script-runtime design
                   called for"
   :kami_clj_play_integration "src/bin/kami_clj_play.rs reuses the deleted
                                original's wgpu rendering/shader/instanced-
                                sprite code, replaced its in-process
                                hecs+CLJ-compile Game struct with a thin
                                KamiHost wrapper loading a pre-compiled
                                game.wasm (disclosed architectural change --
                                the compiler is CLJC now, not callable from
                                Rust at runtime) plus a hand-rolled
                                scene.edn parser scoped to author.clj's
                                known flat output shape (disclosed
                                simplification, not a general EDN reader).
                                Confirmed running: real window, sustained
                                ~60fps for 720 frames, isekai-network's
                                game.wasm executing live (entity count 5->21
                                via spawn, beat-spark bonus firing), pixel-
                                level colour verification via a real
                                render-to-texture screenshot capture
                                (Shiro-hood white near-exact match; teal/lime
                                eye-accent colours consistent with the
                                shader's 0.35*halo glow-brightening + sRGB
                                encode once that two-step colour pipeline
                                was correctly accounted for -- documented as
                                a comment directly in the fragment shader
                                source so future verification work does not
                                repeat the naive-hex-comparison gap)"}
  :naming_review
  {:finding "no established kotoba-lang naming convention was found for
             this exact \"Rust substrate host paired with an almost-same-
             named CLJC sibling repo\" shape at the time of creation --
             kami-script-runtime-rs's own README already flagged this
             explicitly"
   :recommendation "not acted on unilaterally -- renaming a live,
                     manifest-registered, actively-depended-on public repo
                     is a disruptive decision this ADR defers to the
                     project owner. If a convention is later established
                     (e.g. a -rs suffix pattern emerges from other Phase-4/7
                     migration landings), kami-script-runtime-rs is the
                     first known instance and a rename would need a
                     west.yml pin update + isekai-network build-doc update,
                     not a blocking concern today"}
  :genre_base_systems_naming
  {:finding "kami-genre-base-systems' games/<genre>/ layout mirrors
             kami-clj-play/games/<name>/'s established convention exactly
             (author.clj+logic.clj+deps.edn+.gitignore per named game) --
             consistent, no naming gap found"
   :future_question "whether individual genre systems should eventually
                      become separate kami-app-{genre} crates per
                      ARCHITECTURE.md's stated per-game-crate convention is
                      a real open question, deliberately not resolved here
                      -- these are BASE/FOUNDATION systems (pre-crate-worthy
                      prototypes), not shipped games, so the kami-clj-play/
                      games/ precedent (which also holds prototype-tier
                      reference games, not crate-worthy products) is the
                      better-fitting precedent for this repo's current
                      maturity level"}
  :library_reuse_check
  {:finding "none of the ~113 Phase-7-restored kotoba-lang CLJC crates
             (kotoba-lang/physics-2d, /pathfind, /tilemap, etc.) expose
             anything the kami-clj GUEST compiler can call -- they are
             host-side-only Rust-callable libraries (or CLJC ports thereof
             consumed by Rust hosts), not part of the guest module's fixed
             host-import vocabulary. Confirmed, not assumed: the guest
             compiler's callable surface is exactly the 14 host-imports
             kami-script-runtime-rs implements, nothing more, matching this
             session's established finding (concept 2/3/4/5 investigations)
             that guest logic cannot reach Rust-substrate crates directly"}}

 :consequences
 {:isekai_network_unblocked "the specific compile-and-run blocker
                              isekai-network's README documented all
                              session is closed -- real execution, real
                              pixel-verified rendering, both backends
                              deterministic"
  :third_kamiclj_gap_closed "kotoba-lang/engine now has both halves closed
                              (semantic compile + byte emission); the
                              previously-undocumented host-runtime gap
                              (kami-script-runtime's permanent scope
                              exclusion) now has an owner"
  :genre_base_systems_downstream "kami-genre-base-systems (a separate,
                                   parallel initiative this session) can
                                   now, as real follow-up, actually
                                   WASM-compile + run its systems through
                                   this same pipeline -- not attempted in
                                   this ADR's scope, a legitimate next step"
  :naming_debt "one open naming question (kami-script-runtime-rs) is
                explicitly deferred, not silently accepted as final"}

 :alternatives
 [{:option "wait for kotoba-lang/kami-script-runtime's own scope to
            eventually cover the host runtime, rather than creating a new
            repo"
   :rejected_because "that repo's README states the exclusion in absolute,
                       permanent terms -- waiting on a scope change that has
                       already been explicitly ruled out is not a real path"}
  {:option "port the deleted Rust host runtime into kami-engine (the
            asset/contract shell repo) instead of a new repo"
   :rejected_because "kami-engine runs a CI \"no-rust guard\" (confirmed via
                       its own CLAUDE.md) -- structurally cannot host Rust
                       source"}
  {:option "attempt a full hecs-based entity store instead of the
            hand-rolled HashMap store, to match the original more closely"
   :rejected_because "time-boxed given this session's established slow-
                       build cost for wgpu/wasmtime-adjacent Rust crates --
                       the hand-rolled store is real, tested, and
                       functionally equivalent for this session's
                       verification needs; hecs parity is a disclosed,
                       legitimate follow-up, not silently dropped"}]}
